@* M17 (ADR 0118) — the shared bookmark control (the per-target affordance on
   the five detail surfaces). Two states, resolved per target (F1 — "the
   button reflects 'bookmarked' on reload"):
   • **Not bookmarked** (the default) — a "Bookmark" button posting to
     /bookmarks/toggle (the D3 write-lane visibility check + the D4
     idempotency run in the seam at POST — the sole writer, C-M17·1: the
     controller / view never re-derives access).
   • **Bookmarked** — a "Remove" button posting to
     /bookmarks/{kind}/{id}/remove (the D4 physical-removal lane; the row is
     keyed on the owner's own Bookmark row, so the remove needs no target
     read — D5 / F4 — and works even on a dangling row).
   The state is resolved per target by the partial itself, via
   IBookmarkService.IsBookmarkedAsync (the 2026-10-04 F1 amendment, ADR
   0118): a targeted single-row **personal read** (the C-M17·2 shape — no
   AccessAudit row, no CanAsync pass, no target read; the ListAsync
   precedent). The partial is the sole place the state is resolved — it
   makes exactly one such read and calls no other service (the "no
   owner-list load to decide the button's label" pin holds; the read is
   targeted, not the full list). An unsigned-in visitor (no SubjectId)
   resolves NotBookmarked with no query (the C-M17·2 "operator has no read
   standing" gate). Plain form POST (no JS) for both lanes.
   Obs-2 (ADR 0118 amendment, 2026-09-30): each form carries a hidden
   returnUrl input (the surface's own detail URL) so the controller can
   redirect back to the surface the button was clicked on (with the house
   TempData["info"] flash toast), instead of bouncing to /bookmarks. Absent
   returnUrl ⇒ the /bookmarks fallback (the original M17 behavior). *@
@model (string Kind, string Id, string? ReturnUrl)
@using Kumunita.Web.Security
@inject Kumunita.Core.Bookmarks.IBookmarkService Bookmarks
@* The button is pinned to the top-right of the main content card (the render
   site places it first inside .card-body); justify-content-end pushes it to
   the right edge. The owner's per-target bookmark state is resolved once
   here (the partial is the sole resolver — no sibling element, no ViewData
   hand-off, no TagHelper ordering concern) and drives the two lanes below. *@
@{
    var ownerId = User is not null ? KumunitaPrincipal.SubjectId(User) : null;
    var bookmarked = ownerId is not null
        && await Bookmarks.IsBookmarkedAsync(ownerId, Model.Kind, Model.Id);
}
<div class="d-flex justify-content-end mb-3">
    @if (bookmarked)
    {
        @* Bookmarked — the "Remove" lane (D4 physical removal; no target read
           — D5 / F4, keyed on the owner's own row so a dangling row still
           removes). The returnUrl hidden input sends the controller back to
           this surface (the obs-2 redirect-back shape). *@
        <form method="post" action="@($"/bookmarks/{Model.Kind}/{Model.Id}/remove")" style="margin:0;">
            @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
            <input type="hidden" name="returnUrl" value="@Model.ReturnUrl" />
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary">
                <kw-l key="bm.list.unbookmark">Remove</kw-l>
            </button>
        </form>
    }
    else
    {
        @* Not bookmarked (the default) — the "Bookmark" lane (the D3 write-lane
           visibility check + D4 idempotency run in the seam at POST). The
           returnUrl hidden input sends the controller back to this surface
           (the obs-2 redirect-back shape). *@
        <form method="post" action="/bookmarks/toggle" style="margin:0;">
            @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
            <input type="hidden" name="kind" value="@Model.Kind" />
            <input type="hidden" name="id" value="@Model.Id" />
            <input type="hidden" name="returnUrl" value="@Model.ReturnUrl" />
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary">
                <kw-l key="bm.button.bookmark">Bookmark</kw-l>
            </button>
        </form>
    }
</div>
